{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVYdxDQRGACyZRDK2J52","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NaV3JgK8BSnmVx7UWpJepo","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\kver}{\\hat{k}}\n\n\\title{How Many Comparisons Change the Leader? Refit-Verified Influence Perturbations of Bradley--Terry Leaderboards Across Evaluation Designs}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLeaderboards for language models and sports rank participants by fitting a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to pairwise comparisons. It is rarely measured how much of the comparison data a reported ranking depends on. We measure the smallest perturbation we can find and certify that changes the leader or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard. A perturbation either drops comparisons or reverses their outcomes. The search orders comparisons by their first-order influence on the decisive score margin, and an exact refit verifies every reported size. We evaluate 15 cases spanning crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena), expert and GPT-4-judge annotations (MT-Bench), and two NBA seasons. In every design, tens of comparisons change the leader: 62 of 135,622 Arena votes (0.046\\%), 92 expert and 143 GPT-4 judgments, and 7 and 16 NBA games. These sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations, and identical across seeds. The most fragile design depends on the scale and the target. As a fraction of the data Arena is the most fragile; by count, NBA is. Moving a top-$k$ boundary can cost one game, or more than changing the leader. All sizes are upper bounds; cross-design differences are descriptive, not causal.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nPairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n\nWe ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness idea of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We then find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.\n\nOur central finding has two parts. First, in every design we study, tens of comparisons are enough to change the leader. The verified sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations of the same data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}). Second, the question ``which design is most fragile?'' has no scale-free answer. Measured as a fraction of the data, the crowdsourced Arena leader is the most fragile (0.046\\%) and the GPT-4-judge leader the least (5.96\\%). Measured as a count, the NBA leaders are the most fragile (7 and 16 games). The target matters as much as the scale. In both NBA seasons, a single game changes the top-5 set. In MT-Bench, by contrast, the top-3 set costs more than the leader.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_drop_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A refit-verified, influence-ordered protocol for leaderboard fragility. It covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is validated against a planted exact minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n(iii) Three regularities with stated limits. Flipping always needs fewer comparisons than dropping. The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper or dearer to move than the leader. Fragility rankings across designs reverse between the count and fraction scales (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n(iv) An explicit negative result: the search procedure we evaluate does not improve on the plain influence ordering. The pre-declared test of that improvement is unsupported, so we make no algorithmic-superiority claim (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Paired-comparison models and ranking.}\nThe BT model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} and its extensions to ties~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET} underlie most comparison leaderboards. Ties are often handled, as here, by counting them as half wins. Maximum-likelihood fitting is classical~\\citep{Hunter2003MMAF}. Spectral and counting alternatives come with sample-complexity guarantees for recovering the top-$K$ items~\\citep{Negahban2012RankCR,Chen2015SpectralMT,Chen2017SPECTRALMA,Shah2015SimpleRA}. Elo ratings play the same role in sports and games~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. Uncertainty for BT scores is usually quantified by asymptotic theory~\\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI} or the bootstrap~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA}, and Elo-style LLM rankings are known to depend on hyperparameters and match order~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}. These tools answer ``how variable is the ranking under resampling?''. We ask a worst-case, data-specific question: ``how few concrete comparisons does the ranking rest on?''.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and drop-data robustness.}\nInfluence diagnostics go back to Cook's distance~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI}. They were extended to nonlinear models~\\citep{Monari2000WithdrawingAE} and to modern machine learning~\\citep{Koh2017UnderstandingBP}, though their accuracy degrades in strongly non-convex settings~\\citep{Basu2020InfluenceFI}. Infinitesimal-jackknife approximations make leave-out analysis cheap~\\citep{Giordano2018ASA}, and higher-order corrections tighten it~\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}. \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} turn this machinery into a robustness metric, the approximate maximum influence perturbation: the smallest fraction of data whose removal can overturn a conclusion. For least squares, finding the true minimum is computationally hard in general, but it can be certified in low dimensions~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We adopt the drop-data question, apply it to rank conclusions of a BT fit, add an outcome-reversal perturbation, and certify every size by exact refits rather than reporting the linear approximation.\n\n\\paragraph{Manipulation and validity of LLM leaderboards.}\nChatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n\n\\section{Problem Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Data and estimator.}\nA case consists of $n$ comparison rows $r=(a_r,b_r,y_r)$ among $p$ players. The outcome is $y_r\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$: a win for $a_r$, a tie, or a win for $b_r$. The leaderboard is the ridge-penalized BT maximum-likelihood estimate\n\\begin{equation}\n\\hat\\theta=\\arg\\max_{\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^p}\\;\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\Big[y_r\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{a_r}-\\theta_{b_r})+(1-y_r)\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{b_r}-\\theta_{a_r})\\Big]-\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2 ,\n\\label{eq:bt}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 72--117).\n\n\\paragraph{Targets.}\nLet $S$ be the original top-$k$ set under $\\hat\\theta$. The \\emph{top-$k$ target changes} after a perturbation if the refit $\\tilde\\theta$ satisfies $\\max_{j\\notin S}\\tilde\\theta_j>\\min_{i\\in S}\\tilde\\theta_i+\\tau$, with $\\tau=10^{-6}$. Top-1 is the special case $k=1$: some other player overtakes the leader. We study top-1 for every source, plus top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench, which has few players.\n\n\\paragraph{Perturbations.}\nA \\emph{drop} perturbation deletes a set $D$ of rows. A \\emph{flip} perturbation reverses the outcomes of a set $F$ of decided rows ($y\\mapsto 1-y$). Ties cannot be flipped. Drops model missing or filtered data and targeted removal. Flips model mislabeled or adversarially cast votes: the data volume stays the same and only the verdicts change. The quantity of interest is the minimal size $k^\\star$ of a set that changes the target. We report $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$, the size of a set we construct and verify.\n\n\\paragraph{Two size scales.}\nWe report every size both as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$, and we argue that neither alone is adequate. The fraction is the natural robustness scale of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. It answers ``what share of the evidence must be wrong?'', which is the relevant question for random noise and data-quality concerns. The count answers ``how many comparisons must an adversary control?'', which is the relevant question for manipulation, where cost scales with votes, not with shares. The two scales can order designs differently when $n$ varies by two orders of magnitude, as it does here. We treat that disagreement as a finding, not a nuisance (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n\n\\section{Method: Refit-Verified Influence Ordering}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\paragraph{Influence of one row on the decisive margin.}\nLet $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta^{\\text{drop}}_r=-(y_r-p_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}),\\qquad\n\\Delta^{\\text{flip}}_r=(1-2y_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}).\n\\label{eq:influence}\n\\end{equation}\nRows are sorted by ascending $\\Delta_r$, so the rows that shrink the margin most come first. One linear solve gives $v$; the ordering costs $O(p^3+n\\log n)$. Equation~\\eqref{eq:influence} also predicts two qualitative regularities that we test in \\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}. First, for a row won by $a_r$ ($y_r=1$), the flip effect exceeds the drop effect by the factor $1/(1-p_r)\\ge 1$, which equals 2 for an even match-up. Flips should therefore need fewer rows. Second, $|v_{a}-v_{b}|$ shrinks as the information about the $L$--$C$ contrast grows. The \\emph{count} needed should thus scale with the margin times that information, while the \\emph{fraction} also falls when the rows touching $L$ and $C$ are a small share of a large data set.\n\n\\paragraph{Exact verification by prefix search.}\nThe linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$. We then test the target condition. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is about 170 lines of NumPy (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 151--217); it is deterministic, and its seed only initializes an unused random generator.\n\n\\paragraph{What a verified size means.}\n$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial problem; even its least-squares analogue is hard in general~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n\n\\paragraph{Comparators.}\nTwo comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity controls.}\n\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n\n\\paragraph{Data and protocol.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrcl@{}}\n\\toprule\nDesign & Source (pinned snapshot) & $n$ & top-$k$ & What a row is \\\\\n\\midrule\nCrowdsourced votes & Chatbot Arena human preference, 140k release & 135{,}622 & 5 & one user vote \\\\\nExpert annotation & MT-Bench human judgments & 3{,}355 & 3 & one expert judgment \\\\\nLLM judge & MT-Bench GPT-4 pairwise judgments & 2{,}400 & 3 & one GPT-4 verdict \\\\\nSports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\nSports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n\\midrule\nControl & planted three-player case (synthetic) & -- & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\nControl & Arena drop/top-1 with rows permuted & 135{,}622 & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n\\label{sec:res-validity}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. This score is $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$, so a value of 0 means the instrument returned exactly the brute-force minimum. Here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.synthetic_control_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permutation_control_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_match]]\n\nAgainst the random comparator, the influence-ordered sets are far smaller. The mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{rand}}/\\kver)$ is 3.975, with 95\\% CI [3.934, 4.0]. This corresponds to a geometric-mean factor of about $15.7\\times$. The upper end of the interval sits at the clip value of 4, so most cases are censored, and the true advantage is \\emph{at least} this large. Fragility to chosen perturbations is therefore a different quantity from fragility to noise. A leader that survives typical random corruption of tens of comparisons can still fall to a targeted set of the same size.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#random_ratio_x]]\n\nAgainst the direct heuristic, the influence ordering was never worse in the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families: each family score is 0. In the MT-Bench-expert family, the score of $-0.0053$ shows that the heuristic was marginally smaller in at least one case. The evaluator records this only at family level. The ordering therefore improves on the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n\n\\paragraph{No improvement over the plain influence ordering.}\nThe configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Refinements that relinearize the influence after partial removals, or that prune and swap rows within the verified set, were designed but never produced scored evaluations. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.claim_supported]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_amip]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Instrument validation and comparators (sealed evaluation, three seeds). Ratios are mean over the 15 real cases of $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$ with a 95\\% bootstrap CI over cases. Positive values mean the comparator needed more rows.}\n\\label{tab:validity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n\\toprule\nCheck & Result & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nPlanted exact minimum (synthetic, drop/top-1) & $\\kver=8$; score $0$ & returned the brute-force minimum \\\\\nRow permutation (Arena, drop/top-1) & $\\kver=62$ vs.\\ $62$; score $0$ & invariant to row order \\\\\nSeed agreement (101/102/103) & 15/15 real cases identical & deterministic \\\\\nReplay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\nFailed episodes & fraction $0.0$ & every proposal verified \\\\\n\\midrule\nvs.\\ random subsets & $3.975$ [$3.934$, $4.0$] & $\\approx 15.7\\times$ fewer rows (censored) \\\\\nvs.\\ one-step influence ordering & $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] & identical; superiority not supported \\\\\nvs.\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse \\\\\n & MTB-expert $-0.0053$ & heuristic marginally smaller in $\\ge$1 case \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#controls]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Tens of comparisons change the leader in every design}\n\\label{sec:res-main}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fragility_by_design_two_scales.pdf}\n\\caption{Verified perturbation sizes for all 15 real cases, on two scales (log axes). (a) Count $\\kver$ of comparisons dropped or flipped; integers are annotated. (b) The same sizes as a percentage of the source's comparisons, $\\kver/n$. Colors mark the target and perturbation; top-$k$ is top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench (MTB). Sizes are identical across the three seeds.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main} shows the main result. The leader changes after dropping 62 Arena votes, 92 expert judgments, 143 GPT-4 judgments, 7 games of the 2014 NBA season, or 16 games of the 2015 season. Flipping requires 46, 46, 61, 4, and 8 rows, respectively. As fractions, the drop sizes are 0.046\\% of Arena, 2.74\\% of MT-Bench expert judgments, 5.96\\% of GPT-4 judgments, and 0.53\\% and 1.22\\% of the two NBA seasons. The NBA fractions average 0.876\\%.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_drop_frac]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_top1_drop_frac]]\n\nTwo readings follow. First, \\emph{no design is robust in absolute terms.} The largest top-1 set we needed is 143 comparisons, and the smallest is 4 flipped games. For LLM evaluation, these counts are within reach of a single motivated actor casting votes or of a modest annotation error. Second, \\emph{the 135,622-vote Arena leader is not protected by its data volume.} Its 62-vote certificate is a smaller share of the data than any other leader's by more than an order of magnitude. On the fraction scale, Arena's top-1 drop size is $0.046\\%$ against $0.53\\%$ for the most fragile NBA season, roughly an order of magnitude apart. Scale buys precision for the average contrast. It does not protect the specific contrast at the top, where only a small share of rows carries information about the leader and runner-up (Eq.~\\ref{eq:influence}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n\n\\subsection{Drop vs.\\ flip, top-1 vs.\\ top-$k$, and the scale reversal}\n\\label{sec:res-contrasts}\n\n\\paragraph{Flipping is always cheaper than dropping.}\nIn all five sources, the verified flip size for top-1 is smaller than the drop size. The ratio of drop to flip size is 1.35 for Arena, 2.0 for MT-Bench experts, 2.34 for the GPT-4 judge, 1.75 for NBA 2014, and 2.0 for NBA 2015. This matches the first-order prediction of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:influence}. Reversing a verdict both removes evidence for the leader and adds evidence against it, so its effect exceeds that of a drop by the factor $1/(1-p_r)$, which is near 2 for close match-ups. Arena's ratio, the furthest from 2, is not explained by our data. Ties can be dropped but not flipped, and the approximation ignores refitting, so we do not over-interpret it. For manipulation threat models, the flip size is the relevant one: an actor who controls a vote controls its direction.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#drop_over_flip_top1]]\n\n\\paragraph{The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper, or dearer, than the leader.}\nMoving the top-5 set needs 21 Arena votes, against 62 for the leader. In \\emph{both} NBA seasons, a single game suffices. In MT-Bench the top-3 set costs more than the leader: 229 vs.\\ 92 expert judgments and 150 vs.\\ 143 GPT-4 judgments. The cost is governed by the gap at the boundary being tested, not by the leader's lead. In the NBA seasons, the 5th and 6th teams are close enough that one game reorders them. The evaluator's degenerate-margin check rules out exact ties ($m\\le 10\\tau$). So ``is the top-$k$ stable?'' and ``is the leader stable?'' are different questions, and a leaderboard can pass one and fail the other.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#topk_over_top1_drop]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/controlled_contrasts.pdf}\n\\caption{Controlled contrasts. (a) MT-Bench: the same question set judged by expert humans (filled circles) and by GPT-4 (open squares). Values are $\\kver/n$ in percent, with counts annotated. The GPT-4 leader needs a larger share of its data to change for top-1, but a smaller one for the top-3 set. (b) Fragility rank of the drop/top-1 cases by count (left, with $\\kver$) and by fraction (right, with $\\kver/n$). Rank 1 (top) is the most fragile. The ordering of Arena and the NBA seasons reverses between scales (purple).}\n\\label{fig:contrasts}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\nMT-Bench is the closest thing to a controlled comparison in our data: the same benchmark is judged by experts and by GPT-4 (Fig.~\\ref{fig:contrasts}a). For top-1, the GPT-4-judge leader is harder to dislodge, at 143 vs.\\ 92 drops (5.96\\% vs.\\ 2.74\\%) and 61 vs.\\ 46 flips. For the top-3 set the order reverses: 150 vs.\\ 229 drops (6.25\\% vs.\\ 6.83\\%). Neither annotation source is uniformly more robust. The two sources also differ in $n$ (2,400 vs.\\ 3,355), in tie rates, and possibly in which model leads, since player identities are hidden from the finder. The contrast is therefore not a causal estimate of a ``judge effect''.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Fragility rankings reverse between scales.}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:contrasts}b orders the five drop/top-1 cases by count and by fraction. By count, the two NBA seasons are the most fragile (7, 16), followed by Arena (62). By fraction, Arena moves to first place (0.0457\\%), ahead of NBA 2014 (0.531\\%) and NBA 2015 (1.22\\%). The two MT-Bench sources stay last on both scales. A cross-benchmark statement such as ``crowdsourced leaderboards are more fragile than sports tables'' is therefore true on one natural scale and false on the other. Any such comparison must name its scale and justify it by the threat model (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}).\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}\nFor one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, ran in about 16\\,s of finder time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s), because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\nEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\nThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n\n\\paragraph{One estimator, one snapshot per source.}\nAll results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\n\n\\paragraph{Confounded cross-design differences.}\nThe designs differ in $n$, number of players, comparison-graph structure, tie rate, and the size of the decisive margin. With one case per design and target, we cannot attribute differences to the design itself, such as crowd versus judge. The cross-design statements are descriptive.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparator and search coverage.}\nThe direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measured how many comparisons must be dropped or reversed to change a Bradley--Terry leaderboard. The instrument orders comparisons by their influence on the decisive margin and certifies each size by exact refits; on a planted case it recovers the true minimum. Across crowdsourced votes, expert and LLM-judge annotations, and professional sports, tens of comparisons suffice to change the leader. These sets are roughly sixteen times smaller than random perturbations: fragility to chosen data differs from fragility to noise. Cross-design comparisons are scale- and target-dependent. The Arena leader is the most fragile as a share of its data but not as a count. NBA top-5 sets move with one game. MT-Bench top-3 sets resist more than their leaders. Expert and GPT-4 judges trade places between targets. We therefore recommend reporting verified fragility on both scales and for each advertised target. Such reports should be read as upper bounds on a fixed estimator, not as minima or causal properties of an evaluation design. Tighter certificates, data-resampling uncertainty for $\\kver$, and controlled within-design replications are the natural next steps.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\label{app:si}\n\n\\subsection{Per-case verified sizes}\n\\label{app:percase}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The per-seed totals of finder time over all 17 cases were 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Verified sizes per case (identical for seeds 101/102/103). $n$ is derived as in Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}. Percentages are $100\\,\\kver/n$.}\n\\label{tab:percase}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\nSource & Case & $\\kver$ & $n$ & $\\kver/n$ (\\%) \\\\\n\\midrule\nArena (crowd) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 46 & 135{,}622 & 0.0339 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 21 & 135{,}622 & 0.0155 \\\\\nMT-Bench (expert) & drop, top-1 & 92 & 3{,}355 & 2.74 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 46 & 3{,}355 & 1.37 \\\\\n & drop, top-3 set & 229 & 3{,}355 & 6.83 \\\\\nMT-Bench (GPT-4 judge) & drop, top-1 & 143 & 2{,}400 & 5.96 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 61 & 2{,}400 & 2.54 \\\\\n & drop, top-3 set & 150 & 2{,}400 & 6.25 \\\\\nNBA 2014 & drop, top-1 & 7 & 1{,}319 & 0.531 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 4 & 1{,}319 & 0.303 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}319 & 0.0758 \\\\\nNBA 2015 & drop, top-1 & 16 & 1{,}311 & 1.22 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 8 & 1{,}311 & 0.610 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}311 & 0.0763 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSynthetic control & drop, top-1 & 8 & -- & -- \\\\\nPermutation control (Arena) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Recovering $n$ from the evaluation record}\n\\label{app:derivation}\n\nThe evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\texttt{analysis/derive\\_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\texttt{analysis/derived\\_stats.json}.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derive_stats.py]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\subsection{Estimator, cases, comparators and controls in detail}\n\\label{app:details}\n\n\\paragraph{Case construction.}\nFor each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n\n\\paragraph{Random comparator.}\nFor a candidate size $s$, 20 uniformly random subsets of size $s$ are drawn from the perturbable rows, with early stopping. The size counts as changing the target if at least 10 of them change it. The smallest such $s$ is found by doubling and bisection up to half of the perturbable rows. Failure to find one is recorded as $n+1$ (censored). Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\n\n\\paragraph{Direct heuristic.}\nRows where the leader beat the challenger come first, in index order. Then come the leader's other wins, sorted by decreasing fitted strength of the beaten opponent. The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\n\n\\paragraph{Planted control.}\nThree players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair is nearly balanced, with the leader winning by one to three games over half; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70. Before the case is accepted, its exact minimal drop size is found by enumerating all compositions of per-pair winner classes up to size 8. Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\n\n\\paragraph{Scoring.}\nFor real cases, each seed's score is $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(\\min(k_{\\text{infl}},k_{\\text{heur}})/\\kver),-4,4)$. Here $k_{\\text{infl}}$ is the evaluator's one-step-influence comparator, and an unverified proposal scores $-4$. Family scores are weighted means over the family's cases. Because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\text{infl}}$, a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller. The only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only evidence that the heuristic was smaller anywhere.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_score]]\n\n\\subsection{Pre-declared superiority test and unevaluated refinements}\n\\label{app:negative}\n\nThe study pre-declared one inferential claim: the evaluated search would find strictly smaller verified sets than the one-step influence ordering. Specifically, the bootstrap lower bound of the mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{infl}}/\\kver)$ would have to exceed 0. The selected configuration is the plain influence ordering with verified prefix search, so this ratio is 0.0 with CI [0.0, 0.0], and the claim is not supported. The evaluation still completed and qualified on every validity gate. The sizes in this paper are therefore valid measurements, and the negative outcome concerns only algorithmic improvement.\n\nThree refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. All three relinearize the influence after partial removals and exploit the fact that \\eqref{eq:bt} depends only on per-pair counts. The first adds multi-pair targeting and exact pruning. The second adds deterministic refit budgets and a two-for-one swap refinement. The third adds exact pruning seeded from the influence set. None produced a scored evaluation: all three of three were unscored because execution failed before evaluation. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility record}\n\\label{app:repro}\n\n\\paragraph{Data.}\nAll data are public. Arena: \\texttt{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\texttt{lmsys/mt\\_bench\\_human\\_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\texttt{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\texttt{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\texttt{task/resources/final/split\\_manifest.json}.\n\n\\paragraph{Software and compute.}\nPython with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\texttt{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only, two BLAS threads. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Finder time was about 16\\,s per seed; evaluator-side comparators add to this.\n\n\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\nPerturbation finder: \\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree). Evaluator: \\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\texttt{analysis/derive\\_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\texttt{analysis/build\\_figures.py}, which reads only \\texttt{analysis/derived\\_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{app:llm}\n\nLarge language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1422,"cache_read_input_tokens":166819,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1422},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41699338-08fe-4003-85d0-f33b91a46083","uuid":"c1c464a5-b65d-4ee8-8c70-0bcf1765dd41","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:04:41.681Z","request_id":"req_011CgVYduVTCsUzEmakYvwvq"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NaV3JgK8BSnmVx7UWpJepo","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /workspace/paper/paper.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41699338-08fe-4003-85d0-f33b91a46083","uuid":"31738c9d-e3d2-44ab-ad89-559a699e1824","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:04:41.711Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\kver}{\\hat{k}}\n\n\\title{How Many Comparisons Change the Leader? Refit-Verified Influence Perturbations of Bradley--Terry Leaderboards Across Evaluation Designs}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLeaderboards for language models and sports rank participants by fitting a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to pairwise comparisons. It is rarely measured how much of the comparison data a reported ranking depends on. We measure the smallest perturbation we can find and certify that changes the leader or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard. A perturbation either drops comparisons or reverses their outcomes. The search orders comparisons by their first-order influence on the decisive score margin, and an exact refit verifies every reported size. We evaluate 15 cases spanning crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena), expert and GPT-4-judge annotations (MT-Bench), and two NBA seasons. In every design, tens of comparisons change the leader: 62 of 135,622 Arena votes (0.046\\%), 92 expert and 143 GPT-4 judgments, and 7 and 16 NBA games. These sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations, and identical across seeds. The most fragile design depends on the scale and the target. As a fraction of the data Arena is the most fragile; by count, NBA is. Moving a top-$k$ boundary can cost one game, or more than changing the leader. All sizes are upper bounds; cross-design differences are descriptive, not causal.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nPairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n\nWe ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness idea of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We then find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.\n\nOur central finding has two parts. First, in every design we study, tens of comparisons are enough to change the leader. The verified sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations of the same data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}). Second, the question ``which design is most fragile?'' has no scale-free answer. Measured as a fraction of the data, the crowdsourced Arena leader is the most fragile (0.046\\%) and the GPT-4-judge leader the least (5.96\\%). Measured as a count, the NBA leaders are the most fragile (7 and 16 games). The target matters as much as the scale. In both NBA seasons, a single game changes the top-5 set. In MT-Bench, by contrast, the top-3 set costs more than the leader.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_drop_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A refit-verified, influence-ordered protocol for leaderboard fragility. It covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is validated against a planted exact minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n(iii) Three regularities with stated limits. Flipping always needs fewer comparisons than dropping. The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper or dearer to move than the leader. Fragility rankings across designs reverse between the count and fraction scales (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n(iv) An explicit negative result: the search procedure we evaluate does not improve on the plain influence ordering. The pre-declared test of that improvement is unsupported, so we make no algorithmic-superiority claim (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Paired-comparison models and ranking.}\nThe BT model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} and its extensions to ties~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET} underlie most comparison leaderboards. Ties are often handled, as here, by counting them as half wins. Maximum-likelihood fitting is classical~\\citep{Hunter2003MMAF}. Spectral and counting alternatives come with sample-complexity guarantees for recovering the top-$K$ items~\\citep{Negahban2012RankCR,Chen2015SpectralMT,Chen2017SPECTRALMA,Shah2015SimpleRA}. Elo ratings play the same role in sports and games~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. Uncertainty for BT scores is usually quantified by asymptotic theory~\\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI} or the bootstrap~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA}, and Elo-style LLM rankings are known to depend on hyperparameters and match order~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}. These tools answer ``how variable is the ranking under resampling?''. We ask a worst-case, data-specific question: ``how few concrete comparisons does the ranking rest on?''.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and drop-data robustness.}\nInfluence diagnostics go back to Cook's distance~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI}. They were extended to nonlinear models~\\citep{Monari2000WithdrawingAE} and to modern machine learning~\\citep{Koh2017UnderstandingBP}, though their accuracy degrades in strongly non-convex settings~\\citep{Basu2020InfluenceFI}. Infinitesimal-jackknife approximations make leave-out analysis cheap~\\citep{Giordano2018ASA}, and higher-order corrections tighten it~\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}. \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} turn this machinery into a robustness metric, the approximate maximum influence perturbation: the smallest fraction of data whose removal can overturn a conclusion. For least squares, finding the true minimum is computationally hard in general, but it can be certified in low dimensions~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We adopt the drop-data question, apply it to rank conclusions of a BT fit, add an outcome-reversal perturbation, and certify every size by exact refits rather than reporting the linear approximation.\n\n\\paragraph{Manipulation and validity of LLM leaderboards.}\nChatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n\n\\section{Problem Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Data and estimator.}\nA case consists of $n$ comparison rows $r=(a_r,b_r,y_r)$ among $p$ players. The outcome is $y_r\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$: a win for $a_r$, a tie, or a win for $b_r$. The leaderboard is the ridge-penalized BT maximum-likelihood estimate\n\\begin{equation}\n\\hat\\theta=\\arg\\max_{\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^p}\\;\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\Big[y_r\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{a_r}-\\theta_{b_r})+(1-y_r)\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{b_r}-\\theta_{a_r})\\Big]-\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2 ,\n\\label{eq:bt}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 72--117).\n\n\\paragraph{Targets.}\nLet $S$ be the original top-$k$ set under $\\hat\\theta$. The \\emph{top-$k$ target changes} after a perturbation if the refit $\\tilde\\theta$ satisfies $\\max_{j\\notin S}\\tilde\\theta_j>\\min_{i\\in S}\\tilde\\theta_i+\\tau$, with $\\tau=10^{-6}$. Top-1 is the special case $k=1$: some other player overtakes the leader. We study top-1 for every source, plus top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench, which has few players.\n\n\\paragraph{Perturbations.}\nA \\emph{drop} perturbation deletes a set $D$ of rows. A \\emph{flip} perturbation reverses the outcomes of a set $F$ of decided rows ($y\\mapsto 1-y$). Ties cannot be flipped. Drops model missing or filtered data and targeted removal. Flips model mislabeled or adversarially cast votes: the data volume stays the same and only the verdicts change. The quantity of interest is the minimal size $k^\\star$ of a set that changes the target. We report $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$, the size of a set we construct and verify.\n\n\\paragraph{Two size scales.}\nWe report every size both as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$, and we argue that neither alone is adequate. The fraction is the natural robustness scale of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. It answers ``what share of the evidence must be wrong?'', which is the relevant question for random noise and data-quality concerns. The count answers ``how many comparisons must an adversary control?'', which is the relevant question for manipulation, where cost scales with votes, not with shares. The two scales can order designs differently when $n$ varies by two orders of magnitude, as it does here. We treat that disagreement as a finding, not a nuisance (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n\n\\section{Method: Refit-Verified Influence Ordering}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\paragraph{Influence of one row on the decisive margin.}\nLet $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta^{\\text{drop}}_r=-(y_r-p_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}),\\qquad\n\\Delta^{\\text{flip}}_r=(1-2y_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}).\n\\label{eq:influence}\n\\end{equation}\nRows are sorted by ascending $\\Delta_r$, so the rows that shrink the margin most come first. One linear solve gives $v$; the ordering costs $O(p^3+n\\log n)$. Equation~\\eqref{eq:influence} also predicts two qualitative regularities that we test in \\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}. First, for a row won by $a_r$ ($y_r=1$), the flip effect exceeds the drop effect by the factor $1/(1-p_r)\\ge 1$, which equals 2 for an even match-up. Flips should therefore need fewer rows. Second, $|v_{a}-v_{b}|$ shrinks as the information about the $L$--$C$ contrast grows. The \\emph{count} needed should thus scale with the margin times that information, while the \\emph{fraction} also falls when the rows touching $L$ and $C$ are a small share of a large data set.\n\n\\paragraph{Exact verification by prefix search.}\nThe linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$. We then test the target condition. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is about 170 lines of NumPy (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 151--217); it is deterministic, and its seed only initializes an unused random generator.\n\n\\paragraph{What a verified size means.}\n$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial problem; even its least-squares analogue is hard in general~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n\n\\paragraph{Comparators.}\nTwo comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity controls.}\n\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n\n\\paragraph{Data and protocol.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrcl@{}}\n\\toprule\nDesign & Source (pinned snapshot) & $n$ & top-$k$ & What a row is \\\\\n\\midrule\nCrowdsourced votes & Chatbot Arena human preference, 140k release & 135{,}622 & 5 & one user vote \\\\\nExpert annotation & MT-Bench human judgments & 3{,}355 & 3 & one expert judgment \\\\\nLLM judge & MT-Bench GPT-4 pairwise judgments & 2{,}400 & 3 & one GPT-4 verdict \\\\\nSports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\nSports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n\\midrule\nControl & planted three-player case (synthetic) & -- & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\nControl & Arena drop/top-1 with rows permuted & 135{,}622 & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n\\label{sec:res-validity}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. This score is $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$, so a value of 0 means the instrument returned exactly the brute-force minimum. Here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.synthetic_control_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permutation_control_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_match]]\n\nAgainst the random comparator, the influence-ordered sets are far smaller. The mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{rand}}/\\kver)$ is 3.975, with 95\\% CI [3.934, 4.0]. This corresponds to a geometric-mean factor of about $15.7\\times$. The upper end of the interval sits at the clip value of 4, so most cases are censored, and the true advantage is \\emph{at least} this large. Fragility to chosen perturbations is therefore a different quantity from fragility to noise. A leader that survives typical random corruption of tens of comparisons can still fall to a targeted set of the same size.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#random_ratio_x]]\n\nAgainst the direct heuristic, the influence ordering was never worse in the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families: each family score is 0. In the MT-Bench-expert family, the score of $-0.0053$ shows that the heuristic was marginally smaller in at least one case. The evaluator records this only at family level. The ordering therefore improves on the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n\n\\paragraph{No improvement over the plain influence ordering.}\nThe configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Refinements that relinearize the influence after partial removals, or that prune and swap rows within the verified set, were designed but never produced scored evaluations. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.claim_supported]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_amip]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Instrument validation and comparators (sealed evaluation, three seeds). Ratios are mean over the 15 real cases of $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$ with a 95\\% bootstrap CI over cases. Positive values mean the comparator needed more rows.}\n\\label{tab:validity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n\\toprule\nCheck & Result & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nPlanted exact minimum (synthetic, drop/top-1) & $\\kver=8$; score $0$ & returned the brute-force minimum \\\\\nRow permutation (Arena, drop/top-1) & $\\kver=62$ vs.\\ $62$; score $0$ & invariant to row order \\\\\nSeed agreement (101/102/103) & 15/15 real cases identical & deterministic \\\\\nReplay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\nFailed episodes & fraction $0.0$ & every proposal verified \\\\\n\\midrule\nvs.\\ random subsets & $3.975$ [$3.934$, $4.0$] & $\\approx 15.7\\times$ fewer rows (censored) \\\\\nvs.\\ one-step influence ordering & $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] & identical; superiority not supported \\\\\nvs.\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse \\\\\n & MTB-expert $-0.0053$ & heuristic marginally smaller in $\\ge$1 case \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#controls]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Tens of comparisons change the leader in every design}\n\\label{sec:res-main}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fragility_by_design_two_scales.pdf}\n\\caption{Verified perturbation sizes for all 15 real cases, on two scales (log axes). (a) Count $\\kver$ of comparisons dropped or flipped; integers are annotated. (b) The same sizes as a percentage of the source's comparisons, $\\kver/n$. Colors mark the target and perturbation; top-$k$ is top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench (MTB). Sizes are identical across the three seeds.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main} shows the main result. The leader changes after dropping 62 Arena votes, 92 expert judgments, 143 GPT-4 judgments, 7 games of the 2014 NBA season, or 16 games of the 2015 season. Flipping requires 46, 46, 61, 4, and 8 rows, respectively. As fractions, the drop sizes are 0.046\\% of Arena, 2.74\\% of MT-Bench expert judgments, 5.96\\% of GPT-4 judgments, and 0.53\\% and 1.22\\% of the two NBA seasons. The NBA fractions average 0.876\\%.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_drop_frac]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_top1_drop_frac]]\n\nTwo readings follow. First, \\emph{no design is robust in absolute terms.} The largest top-1 set we needed is 143 comparisons, and the smallest is 4 flipped games. For LLM evaluation, these counts are within reach of a single motivated actor casting votes or of a modest annotation error. Second, \\emph{the 135,622-vote Arena leader is not protected by its data volume.} Its 62-vote certificate is a smaller share of the data than any other leader's by more than an order of magnitude. On the fraction scale, Arena's top-1 drop size is $0.046\\%$ against $0.53\\%$ for the most fragile NBA season, roughly an order of magnitude apart. Scale buys precision for the average contrast. It does not protect the specific contrast at the top, where only a small share of rows carries information about the leader and runner-up (Eq.~\\ref{eq:influence}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n\n\\subsection{Drop vs.\\ flip, top-1 vs.\\ top-$k$, and the scale reversal}\n\\label{sec:res-contrasts}\n\n\\paragraph{Flipping is always cheaper than dropping.}\nIn all five sources, the verified flip size for top-1 is smaller than the drop size. The ratio of drop to flip size is 1.35 for Arena, 2.0 for MT-Bench experts, 2.34 for the GPT-4 judge, 1.75 for NBA 2014, and 2.0 for NBA 2015. This matches the first-order prediction of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:influence}. Reversing a verdict both removes evidence for the leader and adds evidence against it, so its effect exceeds that of a drop by the factor $1/(1-p_r)$, which is near 2 for close match-ups. Arena's ratio, the furthest from 2, is not explained by our data. Ties can be dropped but not flipped, and the approximation ignores refitting, so we do not over-interpret it. For manipulation threat models, the flip size is the relevant one: an actor who controls a vote controls its direction.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#drop_over_flip_top1]]\n\n\\paragraph{The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper, or dearer, than the leader.}\nMoving the top-5 set needs 21 Arena votes, against 62 for the leader. In \\emph{both} NBA seasons, a single game suffices. In MT-Bench the top-3 set costs more than the leader: 229 vs.\\ 92 expert judgments and 150 vs.\\ 143 GPT-4 judgments. The cost is governed by the gap at the boundary being tested, not by the leader's lead. In the NBA seasons, the 5th and 6th teams are close enough that one game reorders them. The evaluator's degenerate-margin check rules out exact ties ($m\\le 10\\tau$). So ``is the top-$k$ stable?'' and ``is the leader stable?'' are different questions, and a leaderboard can pass one and fail the other.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#topk_over_top1_drop]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/controlled_contrasts.pdf}\n\\caption{Controlled contrasts. (a) MT-Bench: the same question set judged by expert humans (filled circles) and by GPT-4 (open squares). Values are $\\kver/n$ in percent, with counts annotated. The GPT-4 leader needs a larger share of its data to change for top-1, but a smaller one for the top-3 set. (b) Fragility rank of the drop/top-1 cases by count (left, with $\\kver$) and by fraction (right, with $\\kver/n$). Rank 1 (top) is the most fragile. The ordering of Arena and the NBA seasons reverses between scales (purple).}\n\\label{fig:contrasts}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\nMT-Bench is the closest thing to a controlled comparison in our data: the same benchmark is judged by experts and by GPT-4 (Fig.~\\ref{fig:contrasts}a). For top-1, the GPT-4-judge leader is harder to dislodge, at 143 vs.\\ 92 drops (5.96\\% vs.\\ 2.74\\%) and 61 vs.\\ 46 flips. For the top-3 set the order reverses: 150 vs.\\ 229 drops (6.25\\% vs.\\ 6.83\\%). Neither annotation source is uniformly more robust. The two sources also differ in $n$ (2,400 vs.\\ 3,355), in tie rates, and possibly in which model leads, since player identities are hidden from the finder. The contrast is therefore not a causal estimate of a ``judge effect''.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Fragility rankings reverse between scales.}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:contrasts}b orders the five drop/top-1 cases by count and by fraction. By count, the two NBA seasons are the most fragile (7, 16), followed by Arena (62). By fraction, Arena moves to first place (0.0457\\%), ahead of NBA 2014 (0.531\\%) and NBA 2015 (1.22\\%). The two MT-Bench sources stay last on both scales. A cross-benchmark statement such as ``crowdsourced leaderboards are more fragile than sports tables'' is therefore true on one natural scale and false on the other. Any such comparison must name its scale and justify it by the threat model (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}).\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}\nFor one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, ran in about 16\\,s of finder time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s), because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\nEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\nThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n\n\\paragraph{One estimator, one snapshot per source.}\nAll results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\n\n\\paragraph{Confounded cross-design differences.}\nThe designs differ in $n$, number of players, comparison-graph structure, tie rate, and the size of the decisive margin. With one case per design and target, we cannot attribute differences to the design itself, such as crowd versus judge. The cross-design statements are descriptive.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparator and search coverage.}\nThe direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measured how many comparisons must be dropped or reversed to change a Bradley--Terry leaderboard. The instrument orders comparisons by their influence on the decisive margin and certifies each size by exact refits; on a planted case it recovers the true minimum. Across crowdsourced votes, expert and LLM-judge annotations, and professional sports, tens of comparisons suffice to change the leader. These sets are roughly sixteen times smaller than random perturbations: fragility to chosen data differs from fragility to noise. Cross-design comparisons are scale- and target-dependent. The Arena leader is the most fragile as a share of its data but not as a count. NBA top-5 sets move with one game. MT-Bench top-3 sets resist more than their leaders. Expert and GPT-4 judges trade places between targets. We therefore recommend reporting verified fragility on both scales and for each advertised target. Such reports should be read as upper bounds on a fixed estimator, not as minima or causal properties of an evaluation design. Tighter certificates, data-resampling uncertainty for $\\kver$, and controlled within-design replications are the natural next steps.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\label{app:si}\n\n\\subsection{Per-case verified sizes}\n\\label{app:percase}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The per-seed totals of finder time over all 17 cases were 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Verified sizes per case (identical for seeds 101/102/103). $n$ is derived as in Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}. Percentages are $100\\,\\kver/n$.}\n\\label{tab:percase}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\nSource & Case & $\\kver$ & $n$ & $\\kver/n$ (\\%) \\\\\n\\midrule\nArena (crowd) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 46 & 135{,}622 & 0.0339 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 21 & 135{,}622 & 0.0155 \\\\\nMT-Bench (expert) & drop, top-1 & 92 & 3{,}355 & 2.74 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 46 & 3{,}355 & 1.37 \\\\\n & drop, top-3 set & 229 & 3{,}355 & 6.83 \\\\\nMT-Bench (GPT-4 judge) & drop, top-1 & 143 & 2{,}400 & 5.96 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 61 & 2{,}400 & 2.54 \\\\\n & drop, top-3 set & 150 & 2{,}400 & 6.25 \\\\\nNBA 2014 & drop, top-1 & 7 & 1{,}319 & 0.531 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 4 & 1{,}319 & 0.303 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}319 & 0.0758 \\\\\nNBA 2015 & drop, top-1 & 16 & 1{,}311 & 1.22 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 8 & 1{,}311 & 0.610 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}311 & 0.0763 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSynthetic control & drop, top-1 & 8 & -- & -- \\\\\nPermutation control (Arena) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Recovering $n$ from the evaluation record}\n\\label{app:derivation}\n\nThe evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\texttt{analysis/derive\\_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\texttt{analysis/derived\\_stats.json}.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derive_stats.py]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\subsection{Estimator, cases, comparators and controls in detail}\n\\label{app:details}\n\n\\paragraph{Case construction.}\nFor each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n\n\\paragraph{Random comparator.}\nFor a candidate size $s$, 20 uniformly random subsets of size $s$ are drawn from the perturbable rows, with early stopping. The size counts as changing the target if at least 10 of them change it. The smallest such $s$ is found by doubling and bisection up to half of the perturbable rows. Failure to find one is recorded as $n+1$ (censored). Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\n\n\\paragraph{Direct heuristic.}\nRows where the leader beat the challenger come first, in index order. Then come the leader's other wins, sorted by decreasing fitted strength of the beaten opponent. The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\n\n\\paragraph{Planted control.}\nThree players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair is nearly balanced, with the leader winning by one to three games over half; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70. Before the case is accepted, its exact minimal drop size is found by enumerating all compositions of per-pair winner classes up to size 8. Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\n\n\\paragraph{Scoring.}\nFor real cases, each seed's score is $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(\\min(k_{\\text{infl}},k_{\\text{heur}})/\\kver),-4,4)$. Here $k_{\\text{infl}}$ is the evaluator's one-step-influence comparator, and an unverified proposal scores $-4$. Family scores are weighted means over the family's cases. Because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\text{infl}}$, a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller. The only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only evidence that the heuristic was smaller anywhere.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_score]]\n\n\\subsection{Pre-declared superiority test and unevaluated refinements}\n\\label{app:negative}\n\nThe study pre-declared one inferential claim: the evaluated search would find strictly smaller verified sets than the one-step influence ordering. Specifically, the bootstrap lower bound of the mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{infl}}/\\kver)$ would have to exceed 0. The selected configuration is the plain influence ordering with verified prefix search, so this ratio is 0.0 with CI [0.0, 0.0], and the claim is not supported. The evaluation still completed and qualified on every validity gate. The sizes in this paper are therefore valid measurements, and the negative outcome concerns only algorithmic improvement.\n\nThree refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. All three relinearize the influence after partial removals and exploit the fact that \\eqref{eq:bt} depends only on per-pair counts. The first adds multi-pair targeting and exact pruning. The second adds deterministic refit budgets and a two-for-one swap refinement. The third adds exact pruning seeded from the influence set. None produced a scored evaluation: all three of three were unscored because execution failed before evaluation. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility record}\n\\label{app:repro}\n\n\\paragraph{Data.}\nAll data are public. Arena: \\texttt{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\texttt{lmsys/mt\\_bench\\_human\\_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\texttt{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\texttt{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\texttt{task/resources/final/split\\_manifest.json}.\n\n\\paragraph{Software and compute.}\nPython with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\texttt{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only, two BLAS threads. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Finder time was about 16\\,s per seed; evaluator-side comparators add to this.\n\n\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\nPerturbation finder: \\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree). Evaluator: \\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\texttt{analysis/derive\\_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\texttt{analysis/build\\_figures.py}, which reads only \\texttt{analysis/derived\\_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{app:llm}\n\nLarge language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
