{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVYtbND158Hhh539JkXs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fd4xyy3ErydjqKSdY7NFgC","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'PY'\np='paper.tex'; s=open(p).read()\nR=[\n(\"The finder is about 170 lines of NumPy (\\\\texttt{source\\\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 151--217); it is deterministic, and its seed only initializes an unused random generator.\",\n \"The finder is a single NumPy module (\\\\texttt{source\\\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 151--217). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\"),\n(\"Computing $k^\\\\star$ is a combinatorial problem; even its least-squares analogue is hard in general~\\\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data.\",\n \"Computing $k^\\\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data.\"),\n(\"For least squares, finding the true minimum is computationally hard in general, but it can be certified in low dimensions~\\\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}.\",\n \"For ordinary least squares in low dimensions, such drop-data conclusions can be audited with provable guarantees~\\\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}.\"),\n(\"and higher-order corrections tighten it~\\\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}.\",\n \"and higher-order influence expansions refine it~\\\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}.\"),\n(\"and Elo-style LLM rankings are known to depend on hyperparameters and match order~\\\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}.\",\n \"and the robustness of Elo-style rankings of language models has itself been examined~\\\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}.\"),\n(\" On the fraction scale, Arena's top-1 drop size is $0.046\\\\%$ against $0.53\\\\%$ for the most fragile NBA season, roughly an order of magnitude apart.\", \"\"),\n(\"All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, ran in about 16\\\\,s of finder time per seed (15.5--16.3\\\\,s), because each refit acts on $p\\\\times p$ sufficient statistics.\",\n \"All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\\\times p$ sufficient statistics.\"),\n(\"The per-seed totals of finder time over all 17 cases were 15.99\\\\,s, 15.55\\\\,s, and 16.33\\\\,s.\",\n \"The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\\\,s, 15.55\\\\,s, and 16.33\\\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\"),\n(\"Python with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\\\texttt{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only, two BLAS threads. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Finder time was about 16\\\\,s per seed; evaluator-side comparators add to this.\",\n \"Python with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\\\texttt{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\"),\n(\"One pair is nearly balanced, with the leader winning by one to three games over half; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70.\",\n \"One pair is nearly balanced, with one to three wins above an even split; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in R:\n    assert a in s, a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY","description":"Apply factual-accuracy edits to paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":