{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZ4qqPRLf4zpe7vUDPK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BLT1drAE6w8coswUuBeEtH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport re\np='paper.tex'; s=open(p).read()\n# long file paths -> breakable \\nolinkurl\ndef fix(m):\n    inner=m.group(1)\n    if '/' not in inner: return m.group(0)\n    return '\\\\nolinkurl{'+inner.replace('\\\\_','_')+'}'\ns=re.sub(r'\\\\texttt\\{([^{}]*)\\}',fix,s)\n# Table 1 narrower\ns=s.replace('Chatbot Arena human preference, 140k release','Chatbot Arena human pref.\\\\ (140k)')\ns=s.replace('MT-Bench GPT-4 pairwise judgments','MT-Bench GPT-4 pair judgments')\ns=s.replace('planted three-player case (synthetic)','planted 3-player case (synthetic)')\n# Table 2 narrower\na=s.index('\\\\label{tab:validity}'); b=s.index('\\\\end{tabular}',a)\nt=s[a:b]\nt2=t.replace('\\\\small','\\\\footnotesize').replace('Planted exact minimum (synthetic, drop/top-1)','Planted minimum (synthetic)').replace('Row permutation (Arena, drop/top-1)','Row permutation (Arena)')\nt2=t2.replace('returned the brute-force minimum','equals brute-force minimum').replace('heuristic marginally smaller in $\\\\ge$1 case','heuristic smaller in $\\\\ge$1 case')\nt2=t2.replace('identical; superiority not supported','identical; no superiority').replace('$\\\\approx 15.7\\\\times$ fewer rows (censored)','$\\\\approx 15.7\\\\times$ fewer rows')\ns=s[:a]+t2+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ngrep -n \"nolinkurl\" paper.tex | head -20","description":"Make paths breakable and narrow tables"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2246,"cache_read_input_tokens":208339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2246},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41699338-08fe-4003-85d0-f33b91a46083","uuid":"da1e6e57-75b9-47c2-98ae-601d41803872","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:07:11.692Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZ4nm6DJPpBSbpyVAMh"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BLT1drAE6w8coswUuBeEtH","type":"tool_result","content":"90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 72--117).\n114:The linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$. We then test the target condition. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 151--217). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n120:Two comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n326:The evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n334:For each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n363:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n366:Python with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\nolinkurl{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n369:Perturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree). Evaluator: \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41699338-08fe-4003-85d0-f33b91a46083","uuid":"d3a869e4-79fe-4ddb-8d21-d85a7297c285","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:07:11.786Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 72--117).\n114:The linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$. We then test the target condition. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 151--217). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n120:Two comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n326:The evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n334:For each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n363:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n366:Python with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\nolinkurl{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n369:Perturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree). Evaluator: \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
