{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KcXsmoQ52snT2k2HHoLMVV","type":"tool_result","content":"50:We ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness idea of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We then find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41699338-08fe-4003-85d0-f33b91a46083","uuid":"c3d0fe69-05d2-4eea-9e8b-869f573b6b63","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:07:19.761Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"50:We ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness idea of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We then find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
